<TopicPage slug="subjects/astronomy/expert/cosmology-and-general-relativity">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Cosmology applies general relativity to the universe whole: its geometry, its history, its
    fate. The modern model (ΛCDM) fits an extraordinary range of data with a handful of numbers:
    an expanding universe, 13.8 billion years old, made of ~5% ordinary matter, ~27% dark matter
    and ~68% dark energy. Each of those percentages is measured, not assumed — and two of them
    are, embarrassing and wonderful, unexplained.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the Big Bang case: three independent evidence streams and one outstanding theory" widget="equationStep" config={cosmoCfg} />

  <h2>The deep questions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>What happened before the Big Bang?</strong> Possibly meaningless (time itself may begin there), possibly answered by inflation's precursors, cyclic models or quantum gravity — genuinely open.</li>
    <li><strong>Why is the universe so uniform?</strong> Opposite sides of the sky never had time to touch, yet share the same temperature to 1 part in 100,000. Inflation is the leading explanation; its fingerprints (primordial gravitational waves) are still being hunted in the CMB.</li>
    <li><strong>The Hubble tension:</strong> early-universe and late-universe measurements of the expansion rate disagree by ~9% — small, stubborn, and possibly the first crack in ΛCDM. This is live research, not settled science.</li>
    <li><strong>Fate:</strong> dark energy decides. Continued acceleration → galaxies drift beyond reach and stars eventually burn out (heat-death). If dark energy strengthens → Big Rip. Current data favours the long cold fade.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Lemaître (1927); Planck Collaboration (2018); Riess et al. (Hubble tension measurements); OpenStax, Astronomy 2e (The Big Bang).</p>
</TopicPage>
